 > results.  See the follow-up to the post of mine
 > you quote.

My understanding of the post you answered, to the
contrary, was that he'd chosen his number based not
on "potions generated on the floor" per level but on
"potions seen encountered by the PC, per level, on
average" _in his personal experience_ as a long
term[*] NetHack player and so had included the
factors you think he omitted.

Yes, he did agree later with you that the details of
how the potions encountered distribute with dungeon
levels is a bit glossed over in that estimate, but
so what? There is no real need for a doubly defined
distribution analysis to make the rough level of
estimate he was performing.

In any case, since he showed that changing the
estimate of average number of potions encountered by
20% barely changed the results, I don't think it is
particularly worth arguing over the difference
between an average of four and an average of eight.

If either estimate supports the reasonableness of
claims by others to have encountered games where
insufficient desired potions are encountered, then
the calculation has served its purpose.

         Thus, calling persons who make such claims
